{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target's defense fails to resolve the specific tension in the prompt regarding Popper's own behavior: if corroboration offers no objective reason for preference (being merely a 'conventional' decision postulate), then Popper's personal preference for better-corroborated theories becomes an arbitrary act of will rather than a rational stance. The prompt asks if the preference is defensible 'on Popper's own terms,' and since Popper treated corroboration as a guide to truth-likeness (verisimilitude) rather than mere procedural convention, reducing it to a 'decision postulate' misrepresents his actual philosophical position and fails to answer whether this smuggles induction back in via the concept of verisimilitude.","premise":"Popper's philosophy relies on verisimilitude (truth-likeness) to explain why scientists prefer corroborated theories, not just procedural convention.","premises_essential":["48969427b03779fe5b60e6d65e7ca0dd60ef43fd032d937aa00bf30552f8d043"],"successor_question":"Does appealing to 'verisimilitude' to justify the preference for corroborated theories implicitly reintroduce an inductive assumption about the relationship between past tests and future truth?","target":"48969427b03779fe5b60e6d65e7ca0dd60ef43fd032d937aa00bf30552f8d043"}]}